    "q-32918": {
      "dom_id": "step-q32918",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =c\\,⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =⟨4ct,\\,c{e}^{t},\\,2c{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}=4ct \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}=2c{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} & =1\\cdot 4t \\\\ {t}^{2} & =2t \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{2},\\,{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2(2)^{2},\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
