      "dom_id": "step-q33517",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩ & =c\\,⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t-1}=c{e}^{t-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting $c=1$ into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$, and $t=3.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{3},\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,{e}^{-1}⟩ \\\\ r(3) & =⟨{3}^{3},\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{3-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨27,\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
